      "text": "I want to be straightforward with you here, because accuracy matters for your syllabus.\n\n## An Important Transparency Note\n\nThe tools I have available returned only a brief, partial excerpt from the novel's opening pages and high-level metadata summaries — not enough to give you a fully verified, granular account of the plot mechanics. Critically, **the excerpt tool returned the same opening passage twice**, and my web searches returned only general descriptions without the specific detail you need (the rules of the loop, the full cast of hosts, the Plague Doctor's role, the resolution, etc.).\n\nSome of what I relayed in my previous response — such as specific shortlist details — drew on general knowledge rather than tool-verified sources, and I should be careful not to compound that by constructing a detailed plot summary that might contain inaccuracies about mechanics that are central to your seminar.\n\n---\n\n## What I Can Confirm from Verified Sources\n\nFrom the excerpt and metadata tools, here is what is directly confirmed:\n\n- **The opening:** The novel begins *in medias res* with the narrator disoriented in a forest, unable to remember his own name. A man addresses him as \"Mr. Ashton\" — a name that feels alien to him. This immediately enacts the book's central theme: the protagonist does not know whose body or identity he is inhabiting.\n- **The core conceit:** Aiden Bishop relives the same single day **eight times**, each time inhabiting the consciousness of a **different guest** at the Blackheath estate.\n- **The goal:** He must identify Evelyn Hardcastle's murderer before the day resets, or the loop begins again.\n- **Key themes confirmed:** Recursive narrative, perspective and identity, moral responsibility, the country house mystery tradition, time loops.\n\n---\n\n## My Recommendation\n\nRather than risk giving you inaccurate plot details for a syllabus, I'd suggest one or more of the following:\n\n1. **I can fetch the Wikipedia