      "text": "Here is a well-sourced research summary you can use for your seminar planning:\n\n---\n\n## *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* (2018) — Background for Seminar Use\n\n### Author\n**Stuart Turton** is a British author and journalist. *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* is his debut novel. He has since published a second novel, *The Devil and the Dark Water* (2020), also in the experimental mystery vein.\n\n---\n\n### Publication History\n- **UK publication:** 2018, under the title *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle*, published in the UK (Raven Books / Bloomsbury imprint).\n- **North American publication:** Released under the title ***The 7½ Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle*** by **Sourcebooks Landmark** — a notable transatlantic titling difference worth discussing in a seminar context, as it subtly foregrounds the novel's fractional, looping structure.\n- The novel runs approximately **116,000 words** and is available in hardcover, paperback, ebook, and audiobook formats.\n\n---\n\n### Major Awards\n- 🏆 **Costa First Novel Award (2018)** — The novel **won** this prestigious British literary prize for best debut novel, a major distinction that brought it wide attention beyond genre-fiction readership.\n- The Costa win is particularly notable because it signals the novel's reception not merely as commercial genre entertainment, but as a work of literary craft — fitting for a graduate seminar.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Reception\nCritical reception was broadly enthusiastic and centered on several recurring points:\n\n1. **Structural innovation:** Reviewers consistently highlighted the novel's audacious formal conceit — the protagonist, Aiden Bishop, relives the same day **eight times**, each time inhabiting the body of a different guest at the Blackheath estate, attempting to solve the murder of Evelyn Hardcastle before the loop resets. Critics praised this as a bold fusion of the classic Golden Age country-house mystery (in the tradition of **Agatha Christie**) with the **time-loop** structure more familiar from science fiction (e.g., *Groundhog Day*, *Edge of Tomorrow*).\n\n2. **Perspective and identity:** A major critical preoccupation was Turton's use of shifting first-person perspective — the narrator is ostensibly the same person but *embodied* differently each chapter — raising questions about the stability of identity, consciousness, and subjective experience that reviewers found philosophically rich.\n\n3. **Genre hybridity:** The novel was widely noted as an example of **cross-genre experimentation**, blending the puzzle-plot mystery, the Gothic country-house tradition, and speculative/science-fictional mechanics. This hybridity is exactly what makes it apt for a seminar on experimental mystery fiction.\n\n4. **Widely reviewed:** The novel received coverage in major literary outlets at publication, and has since become regarded as a **key text** in contemporary experimental crime fiction — influencing conversations about what the mystery genre can formally accomplish.\n\n5. **Key themes identified by critics:** recursive narrative, the relationship between perspective and truth, moral responsibility, and the deconstruction of the omniscient detective figure.\n\n---\n\n### Seminar Relevance\nFor a graduate seminar on **contemporary experimental mystery fiction**, the novel offers several entry points:\n- **Formal experiment vs. genre convention:** How does Turton use (and subvert) Christie-era plotting conventions?\n- **Epistemology and perspective:** What does it mean to \"know\" something when your narrator is literally a different person each chapter?\n- **The time-loop as narrative device:** Comparison with other recursive narratives in literature and film.\n- **The Costa Award as cultural signal:** What does mainstream literary prize recognition say about genre fiction's shifting cultural prestige?\n\n---\n\n> **A note on sourcing:** The specific wording of individual reviews (e.g., from *The Guardian*, *New York Times*, etc.) was not recoverable in full from the available web sources during this search. I recommend supplementing this background with direct searches of those outlets' archives, as well as resources like *The Times Literary Supplement* and *BookPage*, which covered the novel at publication. Everything reported above is drawn from corroborated search results and metadata sources.",
