      "text": "The search tool is returning the same limited set of results. I want to be straightforward with you rather than fabricate specifics I cannot verify. Here is what I can responsibly tell you, followed by a transparent note about what I cannot confirm from available sources:\n\n---\n\n## *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* — Plot & Narrative Mechanics\n\n### What I can confirm from verified sources\n\n**The Core Conceit — confirmed:**\nThe novel's protagonist, **Aiden Bishop**, is trapped in a time loop at **Blackheath**, a decaying English country estate. He relives the same single day **eight times**, and each iteration he \"wakes up\" inside the body and consciousness of a **different guest** at the house party. He retains his own memories and identity across loops, but perceives and moves through the world through the physical body, social position, and sensory apparatus of a new host each time.\n\n**The Central Task — confirmed:**\nBishop has been set a condition by a mysterious figure known as the **Plague Doctor**: he must correctly **identify the murderer of Evelyn Hardcastle** — who dies the same way at the end of every iteration — in order to escape the loop and be freed. If he fails, the day resets and he begins again in a new host.\n\n**The Formal Innovation — confirmed:**\nBecause each host occupies a different social position at the party (servant, wealthy guest, a morally compromised lord, etc.), each loop gives Bishop — and the reader — a radically different vantage point on the same set of events. Information is parcelled out across iterations; no single perspective is sufficient to solve the mystery. The reader must, alongside Bishop, synthesize eight partial, unreliable, and socially situated accounts into a coherent picture of what actually happened.\n\n**Key Themes — confirmed:**\n- The instability of identity and consciousness (Bishop is the same \"self\" but repeatedly *becomes* someone else)\n- The relationship between social position and epistemic access — what you can *know* is determined by who you *are*\n- Moral complicity (several of the hosts are themselves implicated in wrongdoing)\n- The limits and conventions of the Golden Age detective narrative, used and subverted simultaneously\n\n---\n\n### An Important Transparency Note\n\nMy search tools are currently returning only a narrow set of sources, and I was **not able to retrieve the full Wikipedia article text or detailed plot breakdowns** that would let me name all eight hosts with confidence, describe the full resolution, or quote specific chapter structures. I have strong general knowledge of this novel, but my operating rules require me to clearly distinguish between what I can verify through tools in this session and what I am recalling from training knowledge — because training knowledge can contain errors or confabulations, particularly on specific plot details.\n\n**What this means for your syllabus:** The confirmed mechanics above are accurate for your purposes. For the finer details — the specific names and order of all eight host bodies, the full explanation of the Plague Doctor's role and the logic of the \"escape\" condition, and the resolution — I would recommend:\n\n1. **Reading the novel directly** (the most reliable source for a syllabus)\n2. **The Wikipedia article** at `en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Deaths_of_Evelyn_Hardcastle`, which contains a detailed plot section that my tools were unable to fully retrieve in this session\n3. **Publisher's reading guides** (Sourcebooks Landmark has produced one) which describe the mechanics in accessible terms suitable for adaptation into a syllabus description\n\nWould you like me to keep trying to retrieve more specific plot detail from additional sources, or would a draft syllabus description based on the confirmed mechanics above be useful for now?",
