      "text": "I want to be fully honest with you here, and I think this response requires a careful, transparent accounting of what my tools have and haven't been able to find.\n\n---\n\n## On the Availability of Scholarly Analysis: An Honest Assessment\n\nAfter extensive searching across multiple query strategies — targeting academic journals (*JSTOR*, *Crime Fiction Studies*, *Journal of Popular Culture*, *Clues: A Journal of Detection*), major literary review outlets (*TLS*, *LARB*, *New Statesman*, *The Guardian*, *The Spectator*, *The Independent*), and general scholarly databases — my search tool has been **consistently returning the same narrow cluster of three results** (Wikipedia, a thin aggregator page, and a publisher page). This is a significant limitation of the search environment I'm operating in during this session, not a reflection of the actual landscape of criticism.\n\nHere is what I can tell you honestly:\n\n---\n\n### What I was able to retrieve\n\nThe one substantive piece of author commentary I retrieved comes from an interview page, which yielded this quote (from `openbooks.net/interviews/stuart-turton`):\n\n> *\"Each host has a different social position, different relationships, different access to information. The puzzle isn't just whodunit — it's about how the same events look completely different depending on who you are and what you're able to see.\"*\n> *\"[I] map[ped] out all eight timelines in parallel before writing a single chapter, to ensure continuity across the loops.\"*\n\nThis is the only direct authorial commentary on the mechanics that my tools surfaced and that I can cite with confidence from this session. It's genuinely useful — it confirms that the voice/perspective shifts are structurally *designed* to enact an epistemological argument, not merely a formal trick — but it is not scholarly analysis.\n\n---\n\n### What I cannot confirm exists (yet) in retrievable form\n\nI was **unable to locate** — through my tools in this session — any of the following:\n- A peer-reviewed journal article dedicated to *Evelyn Hardcastle*'s narrative mechanics\n- A book chapter in a scholarly collection on crime fiction that treats the novel in depth\n- A substantive long-form review in a major literary journal (TLS, LARB, etc.) that I could retrieve and quote\n\nThis does **not** necessarily mean such writing doesn't exist. Given that the novel won the Costa First Novel Award in 2018, it is quite plausible that substantive reviews appeared in outlets like *The Guardian*, *The Times*, *The Spectator*, or *The Sunday Times*, and that academic treatment may have begun to appear in journals like *Clues* or *Crime Fiction Studies* by now. My search tool is simply not surfacing them today.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Search Strategies for You\n\nFor a graduate seminar, here are the most productive avenues to pursue directly:\n\n1. **JSTOR / MLA International Bibliography**: Search `Turton AND \"Evelyn Hardcastle\"` and also broader searches like `\"crime fiction\" AND \"time loop\" AND \"perspective\"` or `\"crime fiction\" AND \"embodiment\" AND \"identity\"`. The novel may appear in articles about contemporary British crime fiction or experimental genre fiction more broadly even when it's not the sole focus.\n\n2. **Crime Fiction Studies** (Edinburgh University Press, launched 2020): This journal is the most likely venue for academic treatment of the novel. Its early issues may include it.\n\n3. ***Clues: A Journal of Detection***: A long-running peer-reviewed journal on mystery fiction, accessible via Project MUSE.\n\n4. **The Guardian Books** (`theguardian.com/books`): Search their archive directly. The Guardian gave the novel significant coverage at publication and their longer reviews often contain substantive literary analysis.\n\n5. **Stuart Turton's own website and interviews**: Beyond the fragment I retrieved, Turton has given multiple interviews to outlets like *The Big Issue*, *CrimeReads*, and *BookPage* in which he discusses the technical challenges of the shifting voice — these would be valuable primary sources for a seminar even if not \"scholarly.\"\n\n6. **CrimeReads.com**: This is probably the best single source for substantive (if not strictly academic) literary analysis of crime fiction. A direct search there for Turton is likely to yield longer analytical pieces.\n\n---\n\n### A Candid Bottom Line\n\nI was not able to deliver what you asked for — specific scholarly or substantive critical essays on the narrative voice and host transitions — because my tools could not retrieve them in this session. I would rather tell you that clearly than construct citations or paraphrase analysis I cannot verify. If you'd like, I'm happy to try fetching specific URLs you already know about, or to discuss the narratological frameworks (focalization theory, embodied cognition, unreliable narration) that *would* be relevant to such an analysis, drawing on well-established critical concepts — clearly flagged as my own analytical framing rather than sourced criticism.",
