nd identify the stylistic markers themselves as part of the exercise — turning the analytical work into the exercise.\n\n---\n\n## What I *Can* Help With\n\nWhile I can't reproduce the prose, I can genuinely help you prepare for this exercise in other ways:\n\n- **A framework of narratological concepts** to apply to the transitions (e.g., Genette's focalization, Cohn's psychonarratology, theories of embodied cognition in fiction) — so you have analytical vocabulary ready for your annotations\n- **A list of specific stylistic features to look for** in each host transition (e.g., changes in diction, physical self-perception, social register, emotional affect, access to information)\n- **A structured annotation template** your students could use to compare the transitions systematically\n- **Discussion questions** designed around the kind of close-reading you're planning\n\nWould any of those be useful as you prepare?",
